1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,000
This text claims it's from the Postal Service asking for a two-dollar redelivery fee.

2
00:00:05,200 --> 00:00:09,000
Is it real? Imposter scams were nearly one in three fraud reports to the FTC in 2025,

3
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,500
accounting for 3.5 billion dollars in reported losses.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:17,500
When you receive a text like this, how do you verify it?

5
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:22,800
The standard advice is: 'contact the company yourself.'

6
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:27,500
But when people paste suspicious messages into an AI chatbot,

7
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:31,000
the model only guesses based on grammar and tone.

8
00:00:31,200 --> 00:00:33,800
Scammers use AI too, so tone proves nothing. Meet Callback.

9
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:38,500
Instead of guessing, Callback runs a live investigation.

10
00:00:38,700 --> 00:00:41,800
We paste the message and click Check it.

11
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,800
Callback resolves who the message claims to be—the US Postal Service—

12
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,200
from a curated list of official organizations, never from the message itself.

13
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:55,500
It compares the message's link against the real domain, usps.com.

14
00:00:55,700 --> 00:01:00,000
The link isn't official, contains a lookalike brand name, and isn't in RDAP.

15
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:03,800
The verdict stamp lands: DOESN'T MATCH.

16
00:01:04,500 --> 00:01:08,200
Callback doesn't just cry scam on everything.

17
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,500
When you receive a real fraud alert from your bank—like Wells Fargo—

18
00:01:12,700 --> 00:01:16,800
it confirms the link is on wellsfargo.com and verifies the phone number

19
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:21,000
against Wells Fargo's own contact page. Green stamp: MATCHES.

20
00:01:21,200 --> 00:01:25,500
That contrast is critical: false alarms make people ignore real warnings.

21
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:30,000
Callback also reads screenshots. Drop an image of a suspicious text,

22
00:01:30,200 --> 00:01:34,800
and Gemini vision reads the message into structured data.

23
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:39,500
Our extraction guard re-scans the text and drops anything the AI hallucinated

24
00:01:39,700 --> 00:01:43,500
that wasn't actually present in the original image.

25
00:01:44,500 --> 00:01:48,200
If someone already clicked the link or replied, panic makes people freeze.

26
00:01:48,400 --> 00:01:53,000
Callback's incident panel gives an immediate action checklist

27
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:57,200
tailored to how they paid, and exports a complete incident summary

28
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:01,500
with exact timestamps and receipts to paste directly into ReportFraud.ftc.gov.

29
00:02:02,500 --> 00:02:06,800
Here is what makes Callback fundamentally different: the AI never decides the verdict.

30
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:11,500
Gemini is used only to read messy text and draft plain-English explanations

31
00:02:11,700 --> 00:02:16,000
where every single sentence must cite an evidence receipt like E1 or it is dropped.

32
00:02:16,200 --> 00:02:19,800
Seven fixed deterministic rules decide whether the channels match.

33
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:24,500
We tested this on 35 synthetic messages modeled on government warnings.

34
00:02:24,700 --> 00:02:29,000
Gemini alone flagged all 20 scams, but it also falsely flagged a real bank alert

35
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:33,000
and gave no evidence. Callback caught 15 scams with zero false alarms,

36
00:02:33,200 --> 00:02:37,500
cited evidence on 74 out of 80 sentences, and answered 'can't verify' on 28.6%.

37
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:42,200
Callback is honest about its limits. It focuses on US numbers and 27 curated orgs.

38
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:47,000
Official phone numbers can only be confirmed where organizations publish them.

39
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:51,500
If an entity is unlisted or blocks scraping, Callback says 'can't verify' instead of guessing.

40
00:02:52,200 --> 00:02:56,000
I'm John Tewolde, and this is Callback.

41
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:59,800
Try it live at callback-lac.vercel.app. Don't trust the number in the message. Callback finds the real one.
